INET(3)                        P32W Unix                         INET(3)

NAME
       inet, gethostbyname, inet_addr, inet_ntoa, htons - addresses

SYNOPSIS
       #include <netdb.h>
       #include <arpa/inet.h>

       struct hostent *gethostbyname(const char *name);
       const char *hstrerror(int err);
       extern int h_errno;
       in_addr_t inet_addr(const char *s);
       int inet_aton(const char *s, struct in_addr *a);
       char *inet_ntoa(struct in_addr a);
       unsigned short htons(unsigned short v);   (and ntohs)
       unsigned htonl(unsigned v);               (and ntohl)

DESCRIPTION
       gethostbyname gives a host's address: h_addr (the first of
       h_addr_list) is 4 bytes in network order, ready for a
       sockaddr_in. A dotted address ("10.0.2.2") is taken as it
       is; a name is looked up by the network coprocessor's DNS
       server (netctl(2)). On failure it returns null and sets
       h_errno: HOST_NOT_FOUND, TRY_AGAIN or NO_RECOVERY, which
       hstrerror describes.

       inet_aton reads a dotted address into *a (1, or 0 if s is
       not one); inet_addr returns it, or INADDR_NONE. inet_ntoa
       writes one in a static buffer.

       htons and htonl turn a 16- or 32-bit number into network
       byte order (big-endian: P32W is little-endian, so they swap
       the bytes); ntohs and ntohl turn it back.

SEE ALSO
       socket(2), netctl(2)

BUGS
       No /etc/hosts, and one address per name. The result is
       static: the next call overwrites it.
